Good. I think we now have the core principles sufficiently nailed down that we should freeze those decisions before writing the database schema.

The important rules we've established are:

Every asset is independently versioned.

[AMENDED 2026-10-01 — see docs/uploadmodel01. The two rules below described
 upload-driven versioning and are no longer the model. An upload no longer
 creates a version: the developer owns versioning in their sandbox, and an
 upload record is a HISTORY TRAIL entry rather than a storage event. An upload
 that adds no new bytes is still recorded, because it documents what was
 submitted, under what name, and when. Version rules only change when an asset
 moves into game space.]

  (was) Uploading changed content creates the next version.
  (was) Uploading identical content does not create a pointless new version.

  (now) An upload records what the developer submitted. The SHA store gains an
        entry only for content it does not already hold; known content is
        referenced, not stored. An upload of entirely known content is
        recorded with no new SHAs, after warning the developer.

Files are identified by SHA-256 so identical physical content can be stored only once.
Different assets may legitimately reference the same physical file.
A control file is never automatically modified by the repository.
The sandbox is responsible for discovering actual dependencies.
Missing dependencies are reported, with as much diagnostic information as required.
If an apparently missing dependency has identical content elsewhere, the system can report the possible match.
The developer decides whether to change the control file.
A successful test records the exact asset versions/dependencies and game build involved.
Tests remain historical and immutable.
The "current game version" is a pointer to a specific tested/approved version, not simply the newest version.





